<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<chapter xml:id="matrices"><title>Matrix exponentials and applications</title>
<section xml:id="series"><title>The matrix exponential series</title>
<p>Let <m>\mathbb F=\mathbb R</m> or <m>\mathbb C</m>. Write <m>A^*</m> for conjugate transpose. The Hilbert--Schmidt norm is <m>\|A\|_{\mathrm{HS}}=(\sum_{i,j}|a_{ij}|^2)^{1/2}</m>; the inner product convention <m>\langle A,B\rangle=\operatorname{tr}(AB^*)</m> is linear in its first argument.</p>
<proposition xml:id="norm"><statement><p>For square matrices, <m>\|AB\|_{\mathrm{HS}}\leq\|A\|_{\mathrm{HS}}\|B\|_{\mathrm{HS}}</m>.</p></statement><proof><p>Apply Cauchy--Schwarz to each entry:</p><p><me>\sum_{i,k}\left|\sum_j a_{ij}b_{jk}\right|^2\leq\sum_{i,k}\left(\sum_j|a_{ij}|^2\right)\left(\sum_j|b_{jk}|^2\right)=\|A\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2\|B\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2.</me></p></proof></proposition>
<definition><statement><p>The <term>matrix exponential</term> is</p><p><me>e^A=\sum_{m=0}^{\infty}\frac{A^m}{m!},\qquad A^0=I.</me></p></statement></definition>
<proposition xml:id="series-derivative"><statement><p>The series defining <m>e^{tA}</m> converges absolutely and uniformly on bounded real intervals, as do its termwise derivatives of every order. In particular,</p><p><me>\frac{d}{dt}e^{tA}=Ae^{tA}=e^{tA}A.</me></p></statement><proof><p>For <m>m\geq1</m>, <xref ref="norm"/> implies <m>\|A^m\|\leq\|A\|^m</m>. On <m>|t|\leq T</m>, the positive-degree terms are dominated by <m>\sum_{m\geq1}T^m\|A\|^m/m!</m>. The constant term has norm <m>\|I\|_{\mathrm{HS}}=\sqrt n</m> and is treated separately. The <m>k</m>th derivative terms, for <m>m\geq k\geq1</m>, are bounded by <m>\|A\|^k(T\|A\|)^{m-k}/(m-k)!</m>. Uniform convergence justifies differentiation and index shifting.</p></proof></proposition>
<proposition xml:id="commuting"><title>Commuting matrices</title><statement><p>If <m>AB=BA</m>, then <m>e^{A+B}=e^Ae^B</m>. Consequently <m>(e^A)^{-1}=e^{-A}</m>.</p></statement><proof><p>The commuting binomial theorem and absolute convergence permit rearrangement:</p><p><me>e^{A+B}=\sum_{m\geq0}\sum_{k=0}^m\frac{A^kB^{m-k}}{k!(m-k)!}=\left(\sum_{k\geq0}\frac{A^k}{k!}\right)\left(\sum_{r\geq0}\frac{B^r}{r!}\right).</me></p><p>Indeed, submultiplicativity bounds the sum of norms by the product of the two convergent norm series. Take <m>B=-A</m> for the inverse formula.</p></proof></proposition>
<proposition xml:id="det-exp"><title>Trace and determinant</title><statement><p>For real or complex square matrices, <m>\det(e^A)=e^{\operatorname{tr}A}</m>.</p></statement><proof><p>Over <m>\mathbb C</m>, choose an invertible <m>S</m> such that <m>T=S^{-1}AS</m> is upper triangular, with diagonal <m>\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_n</m>. Termwise conjugation gives <m>e^T=S^{-1}e^AS</m>. The matrix <m>e^T</m> is upper triangular with diagonal <m>e^{\lambda_1},\ldots,e^{\lambda_n}</m>. Thus <m>\det(e^A)=\prod_je^{\lambda_j}=e^{\operatorname{tr}A}</m>. Real matrices are included by complexification.</p></proof></proposition>
</section>
<section xml:id="identification"><title>Agreement with the Lie group exponential</title>
<theorem xml:id="matrix-exp"><statement><p>Under <m>\operatorname{Lie}(GL(n,\mathbb F))=M_n(\mathbb F)</m>, the Lie group exponential is the matrix series: <m>\exp_{GL(n,\mathbb F)}(X)=e^X</m>.</p></statement><proof><p>Differentiating left multiplication gives <m>\widetilde X_g=gX</m>. The curve <m>\alpha(t)=e^{tX}</m> takes values in <m>GL(n,\mathbb F)</m> by <xref ref="commuting"/>, has <m>\alpha(0)=I</m>, and satisfies <m>\alpha'(t)=e^{tX}X=\widetilde X_{\alpha(t)}</m> by <xref ref="series-derivative"/>. Uniqueness identifies it with the integral curve used to define <m>\exp</m>.</p></proof></theorem>
<corollary xml:id="representations"><title>Representations</title><statement><p>For a smooth representation <m>\pi:G\to GL(V)</m> on a finite-dimensional vector space,</p><p><me>\pi(\exp_GX)=e^{d\pi_e(X)},\qquad d\pi_e(X)=\left.\frac{d}{dt}\right|_{t=0}\pi(\exp_G(tX)).</me></p></statement><proof><p>Apply <xref ref="natural"/> and <xref ref="matrix-exp"/>. The derivative formula follows by the chain rule.</p></proof></corollary>
<example><title>Rotations and shears (supplementary)</title><p>For <m>J=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;-1\\1&amp;0\end{pmatrix}</m>, the relation <m>J^2=-I</m> gives</p><p><me>e^{tJ}=\begin{pmatrix}\cos t&amp;-\sin t\\\sin t&amp;\cos t\end{pmatrix}.</me></p><p>For <m>N=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1\\0&amp;0\end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>N^2=0</m> gives <m>e^{tN}=I+tN</m>. The same construction therefore describes periodic rotations and nonperiodic shears.</p></example>
</section>
<section xml:id="classical"><title>Lie algebras of matrix groups</title>
<example xml:id="sl"><title>The special linear group</title><p>By <xref ref="subgroup-test"/> and <xref ref="det-exp"/>, a real matrix <m>X</m> belongs to <m>\mathfrak{sl}(n,\mathbb R)</m> exactly when <m>e^{t\operatorname{tr}X}=1</m> for every real <m>t</m>, which is equivalent to <m>\operatorname{tr}X=0</m>. Thus</p><p><me>\mathfrak{sl}(n,\mathbb R)=\{X\in M_n(\mathbb R):\operatorname{tr}X=0\}.</me></p><p>The same result holds over <m>\mathbb C</m>, by differentiating <m>e^{t\operatorname{tr}X}=1</m> at <m>t=0</m>.</p></example>
<example xml:id="so"><title>The orthogonal group</title><p>If <m>e^{tX}\in O(n)</m> for all <m>t</m>, differentiation of <m>(e^{tX})^Te^{tX}=I</m> at zero gives <m>X^T+X=0</m>. Conversely, if <m>X^T=-X</m>, termwise transposition and <xref ref="commuting"/> give <m>(e^{tX})^Te^{tX}=e^{-tX}e^{tX}=I</m>. Therefore</p><p><me>\mathfrak{so}(n)=\operatorname{Lie}(O(n))=\{X\in M_n(\mathbb R):X^T=-X\}.</me></p><p>Since the trace of a skew-symmetric matrix is zero, these exponentials have determinant one; hence <m>SO(n)</m> has the same Lie algebra. There are <m>n(n-1)/2</m> free entries above the diagonal.</p></example>
<example><title>The unitary group (supplementary)</title><p>Replacing transpose by conjugate transpose in the preceding argument gives</p><p><me>\mathfrak u(n)=\{X\in M_n(\mathbb C):X^*=-X\},\qquad \mathfrak{su}(n)=\{X:X^*=-X,\ \operatorname{tr}X=0\}.</me></p><p>These are real Lie algebras; in general they are not complex vector subspaces.</p></example>
</section>
<exercises xml:id="exercises"><title>Exercises (supplementary)</title>
<exercise xml:id="ex-additive"><statement><p>Compute the exponential map of the additive group <m>\mathbb R^n</m> and of the multiplicative group <m>\mathbb R_{&gt;0}</m>. Identify their kernels and images.</p></statement><solution><p>They are <m>X\mapsto X</m> and <m>x\mapsto e^x</m>. Both have trivial kernel and are surjective onto their stated groups.</p></solution></exercise>
<exercise xml:id="ex-conjugation"><statement><p>Prove <m>e^{SAS^{-1}}=Se^AS^{-1}</m> directly from the series. Deduce the corresponding formula for the exponential on <m>GL(V)</m> under change of basis.</p></statement><hint><p>First show <m>(SAS^{-1})^m=SA^mS^{-1}</m>, including <m>m=0</m>, and pass to the limit.</p></hint></exercise>
<exercise xml:id="ex-noncommuting"><statement><p>Set <m>A=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1\\0&amp;0\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>B=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;0\\1&amp;0\end{pmatrix}</m>. Compute <m>e^Ae^B</m> and <m>e^{A+B}</m>, and show that they differ.</p></statement><solution><p>The first is <m>\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;1\\1&amp;1\end{pmatrix}</m>. Since <m>(A+B)^2=I</m>, the second is <m>\begin{pmatrix}\cosh1&amp;\sinh1\\\sinh1&amp;\cosh1\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p></solution></exercise>
<exercise xml:id="ex-products"><statement><p>Show that every element of a connected Lie group is a finite product of exponentials. Explain why this does not imply that every element is a single exponential.</p></statement><hint><p>Use the open subgroup generated by a symmetric identity neighborhood in the proof of <xref ref="uniqueness"/>. A product of exponentials cannot in general be combined by adding their exponents.</p></hint></exercise>
<exercise xml:id="ex-not-surjective"><statement><p>Show that <m>\operatorname{diag}(-1,-2)</m> is not <m>e^A</m> for any real <m>2\times2</m> matrix <m>A</m>, although it lies in the identity component of <m>GL(2,\mathbb R)</m>.</p></statement><hint><p>If <m>e^A=D</m>, then <m>A</m> commutes with <m>D</m>. A matrix commuting with this diagonal matrix with distinct diagonal entries is itself diagonal. For the component claim, join <m>I</m> to <m>-I</m> by rotations, then join <m>-I</m> to <m>D</m> by <m>\operatorname{diag}(-1,-(1+s))</m>, <m>0\leq s\leq1</m>.</p></hint></exercise>
<exercise xml:id="ex-unitary"><statement><p>Supply both directions of the proof for <m>\mathfrak u(n)</m> and <m>\mathfrak{su}(n)</m>. Compute their real dimensions.</p></statement><solution><p>Differentiation at zero gives <m>X^*+X=0</m> and, for the special unitary group, <m>\operatorname{tr}X=0</m>. Conversely these identities imply unitarity and determinant one of <m>e^{tX}</m>. A skew-Hermitian matrix has <m>n</m> imaginary diagonal parameters and <m>2\binom n2</m> real parameters off the diagonal, giving dimensions <m>n^2</m> and <m>n^2-1</m>.</p></solution></exercise>
</exercises>
</chapter>

